{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgtJbt33YeDR4gvjMtXJb","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"The screen does not single out a few repetition neurons. Every layer has dozens to hundreds of neurons with |d| > 1.5, and the maximum effect sizes are similar everywhere (2.2–3.1). That is what you'd expect when the activations are averaged over thousands of tokens of homogeneous looped text: the mean activation mostly reflects what the text is about, not what produces the loop. So I can't localize from these means, and need per-token evidence. Before designing that pass, I want to understand the stopping side. How do the 64 finished answers end their thinking, and do the cut answers ever write a concluding statement or a `\\boxed{}` inside the thinking without closing it? If cut texts often state a final answer and then keep going, part of the failure is a broken transition to `</think>`. If they rarely conclude, the loop is pre-empting the conclusion.\n\n```python\n# How does thinking end in finished answers, and do cut answers 'conclude' inside thinking without closing?\nfin=[(r['id'],s['text']) for r in rows for s in r['samples'] if not s['cut']]\nfor i,t in fin[:6]:\n    k=t.find('</think>'); pre=t[max(0,k-220):k].replace('\\n','/')\n    print(i[:30], 'think_end_char',k,'::', pre.encode('ascii','replace').decode())\ncnt=collections.Counter()\nfor i,t in cut_samples:\n    cnt['boxed_in_text']+= ('\\\\boxed' in t)\n    cnt['final_answer_phrase']+= bool(re.search(r'[Ff]inal [Aa]nswer', t))\n    cnt['the_answer_is']+= bool(re.search(r'answer is', t))\nprint(dict(cnt), 'of', len(cut_samples))\nfin_think_len=[t.find('</think>') for i,t in fin]\nprint('finished: chars to </think> quantiles', np.quantile(fin_think_len,[.1,.5,.9]))\n```"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":2410,"cache_read_input_tokens":61314,"c